1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,300
Strong, enough to survive a fiery launch.

2
00:00:04,920 --> 00:00:08,760
Durable, enough to operate in the hostile environment of space.

3
00:00:09,640 --> 00:00:14,980
Powerful, enough to photograph a softball from an orbit hundreds of miles away.

4
00:00:15,900 --> 00:00:22,880
Their development is a grand saga of competition, politics, and technology taken to the very limit.

5
00:00:24,440 --> 00:00:28,300
Now, satellites on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
silently more than 500 of them orbit our earth

7
00:00:45,880 --> 00:00:51,820
it's hard to imagine a world where we don't have satellites

8
00:00:51,821 --> 00:00:58,320
it has literally changed the way in which we live since the mid-1960s

9
00:00:58,321 --> 00:01:04,440
Today's high-tech satellites are some of the most complex machines ever invented.

10
00:01:04,680 --> 00:01:08,960
They are the crucial link in an ever-increasing number of tasks,

11
00:01:09,420 --> 00:01:14,620
such as weather forecasting, global telephone, fax and television communication,

12
00:01:15,480 --> 00:01:18,460
military and commercial reconnaissance, and even navigation.

13
00:01:19,420 --> 00:01:23,780
But just over 50 years ago, satellites were simply an idea

14
00:01:23,781 --> 00:01:28,540
in the mind of a British scientist famous for his science fiction writing.

15
00:01:30,920 --> 00:01:31,620
Arthur C.

16
00:01:31,621 --> 00:01:35,900
Clarke was the first man to envision many of the modern applications of satellites.

17
00:01:36,440 --> 00:01:39,220
He also penned 2001 A Space Odyssey.

18
00:01:40,220 --> 00:01:44,500
And in 1945, 12 years before Sputnik's launch,

19
00:01:44,501 --> 00:01:47,560
he wrote an article in Wireless World magazine

20
00:01:47,561 --> 00:01:51,480
describing how satellites could be placed in geostationary orbit

21
00:01:51,481 --> 00:01:53,580
and used to relay signals.

22
00:01:53,780 --> 00:01:58,780
Today, most communication satellites do operate in this orbit,

23
00:01:58,781 --> 00:02:03,780
rotating at the same rate as the Earth because of their location,

24
00:02:03,781 --> 00:02:07,780
22,300 miles above the equator.

25
00:02:07,781 --> 00:02:12,780
In addition, Clark foresaw polar orbiting and weather satellites

26
00:02:12,781 --> 00:02:15,780
and advocated an orbiting space telescope,

27
00:02:15,781 --> 00:02:19,780
which today exists as NASA's Hubble telescope.

28
00:02:19,781 --> 00:02:30,920
So if any person had the most ideas at the earliest time about the modern applications of satellites, Arthur Clarke was the one.

29
00:02:31,580 --> 00:02:40,020
Arthur Clarke was one of a handful of people at that time who had confidence that man-made objects would one day circle the planet.

30
00:02:40,021 --> 00:02:53,620
Satellites have to be propelled at very high speeds in order to avoid being pulled back to Earth.

31
00:02:54,340 --> 00:03:02,180
For example, the faster a bullet is fired from a rifle, the further it travels before being pulled back to Earth by gravity.

32
00:03:03,080 --> 00:03:08,880
But theoretically, if a bullet could be fired at 17,500 miles an hour,

33
00:03:09,240 --> 00:03:11,680
it would circle the planet as a satellite.

34
00:03:13,740 --> 00:03:18,780
Today, only rockets can propel something at this very high speed,

35
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:21,360
equal to about 5 miles per second.

36
00:03:21,860 --> 00:03:26,280
You have to have a propulsion unit which will take the thing up

37
00:03:26,281 --> 00:03:31,180
and put it into a flight path generally parallel to the Earth.

38
00:03:31,181 --> 00:03:35,280
Now, of course, gravity is going to try to pull that down.

39
00:03:35,281 --> 00:03:45,500
But if you put this at high enough speed, you generate enough centrifugal force that it counteracts the force of gravity trying to pull it down.

40
00:03:45,960 --> 00:03:54,780
In other words, the amount that the satellite is pulled closer to Earth by gravity is canceled by the curvature of the Earth away from the speeding spacecraft.

41
00:03:54,781 --> 00:04:01,200
It continually falls around the Earth instead of falling down to the surface.

42
00:04:01,980 --> 00:04:05,480
This is the principle that guided Arthur C. Clarke's reasoning.

43
00:04:07,840 --> 00:04:10,700
Once a satellite is released from the main rocket,

44
00:04:10,840 --> 00:04:16,500
small booster rockets adjust the satellite's orientation to point it towards the correct spot on Earth.

45
00:04:17,800 --> 00:04:21,340
And since there is no atmosphere in space to slow the orbiter down,

46
00:04:21,341 --> 00:04:27,140
the rockets only need to fire if the satellite slightly deviates from its charted course.

47
00:04:29,440 --> 00:04:35,460
However, just because the theory behind turning an object into an Earth satellite seems simple

48
00:04:35,461 --> 00:04:38,820
does not mean we can launch anything we want into orbit.

49
00:04:41,200 --> 00:04:44,460
Weight and cost have always been concerns.

50
00:04:45,320 --> 00:04:48,040
Some of the first satellites weighed only a few pounds

51
00:04:48,041 --> 00:04:52,000
but still cost millions of dollars to build and send into space.

52
00:04:53,060 --> 00:04:57,300
Today, thanks to more powerful rockets, satellites can weigh more than a ton.

53
00:04:57,880 --> 00:05:01,820
But launching is still not cheap, at $30,000 per pound.

54
00:05:03,160 --> 00:05:07,200
The ultimate extensions of size and weight in satellite technology

55
00:05:07,201 --> 00:05:11,380
are the manned space stations where astronauts can live for months at a time

56
00:05:11,381 --> 00:05:12,940
while circling the planet.

57
00:05:12,941 --> 00:05:24,700
Currently, several countries, including the United States and Russia, are working together to build a new international space station.

58
00:05:25,500 --> 00:05:32,760
Yet for much of the world's history in space, cooperation was the furthest thing from the minds of the leaders of these two superpowers.

59
00:05:32,761 --> 00:05:46,761
Prosperity and national confidence were running rampant in 1950s America.

60
00:05:48,080 --> 00:05:53,700
Dwight Eisenhower, the leader of the Allied army that won World War II, was in the White House.

61
00:05:54,640 --> 00:06:00,000
Tail fins, malt shops, and Elvis Presley symbolized the enthusiasm of a new era.

62
00:06:00,860 --> 00:06:05,100
American consumers reveled in unparalleled materialism.

63
00:06:07,220 --> 00:06:09,840
The Soviet Union was a concern,

64
00:06:10,340 --> 00:06:12,940
but America had been first to develop the atom bomb,

65
00:06:13,040 --> 00:06:17,560
and most citizens assumed that the U.S. was still the world's technological leader.

66
00:06:18,320 --> 00:06:21,500
Until October 4, 1957,

67
00:06:22,140 --> 00:06:25,500
when the Soviet Union launched something they called Sputnik,

68
00:06:26,060 --> 00:06:27,560
meaning traveling companion.

69
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:33,760
My own personal reaction was, gee, that's great.

70
00:06:34,500 --> 00:06:40,120
But the general public, of course, raised a hue and cry that we somehow let the Russians get ahead.

71
00:06:40,960 --> 00:06:45,740
The successful operation of Sputnik is the world's first artificial satellite,

72
00:06:46,220 --> 00:06:49,700
dealt a sledgehammer blow to America's national pride.

73
00:06:50,360 --> 00:06:55,860
It was simply a 184-pound test payload containing a radio beacon and a thermometer.

74
00:06:55,861 --> 00:07:02,860
The beacon was used to prove that artificial objects in outer space could be tracked from Earth.

75
00:07:02,861 --> 00:07:09,860
But even though the technology was fairly simple, its impact was enormous.

76
00:07:09,861 --> 00:07:15,860
Many people worried that if the Soviet Union had the rocket technology to launch something into space,

77
00:07:15,861 --> 00:07:22,860
there was nothing stopping them from launching nuclear warheads capable of reaching America,

78
00:07:22,861 --> 00:07:25,840
an attack which the U.S. could not counter.

79
00:07:27,420 --> 00:07:30,060
Yet for all the fear that Sputnik raised,

80
00:07:30,160 --> 00:07:33,780
there were actually several positive benefits for the United States.

81
00:07:36,160 --> 00:07:41,220
The Soviets really did us a very big favor by launching Sputnik first

82
00:07:41,221 --> 00:07:43,940
because they eliminated any legal challenge

83
00:07:43,941 --> 00:07:46,720
to overflight of territory with a satellite

84
00:07:46,721 --> 00:07:49,360
because they essentially did it themselves

85
00:07:49,361 --> 00:07:51,220
and so they would have a hard time complaining

86
00:07:51,221 --> 00:07:53,260
about anything that we did after that.

87
00:07:54,160 --> 00:07:57,540
Millions of dollars began to pour into the coffers

88
00:07:57,541 --> 00:07:59,660
of the fledgling U.S. space program.

89
00:08:00,300 --> 00:08:04,400
Clearly, America did not enjoy being second best to anyone.

90
00:08:05,140 --> 00:08:07,360
The space race was on.

91
00:08:08,660 --> 00:08:11,600
At the time of the successful Sputnik launch,

92
00:08:11,820 --> 00:08:14,520
the U.S. had already been working for several years

93
00:08:14,521 --> 00:08:16,940
on the development of its own satellites.

94
00:08:18,600 --> 00:08:20,540
Initially, many people in the government

95
00:08:20,541 --> 00:08:23,020
weren't convinced about their usefulness.

96
00:08:23,660 --> 00:08:25,380
In fact, before Sputnik was launched,

97
00:08:25,800 --> 00:08:27,820
American proponents of space operations

98
00:08:27,821 --> 00:08:29,960
were still struggling for respect.

99
00:08:32,500 --> 00:08:36,680
We were considered, frankly, a bunch of crackpots and nuts.

100
00:08:37,480 --> 00:08:39,300
One time after I'd given a lecture

101
00:08:39,301 --> 00:08:41,280
and had been quoted in a newspaper

102
00:08:41,281 --> 00:08:44,420
about military use of satellites for reconnaissance

103
00:08:44,421 --> 00:08:45,600
and how you could counter them,

104
00:08:46,200 --> 00:08:49,860
this very well-meaning senior man came up to me and said,

105
00:08:49,861 --> 00:08:54,640
you've got to stop talking like that because you are destroying your reputation.

106
00:08:55,320 --> 00:08:58,260
Sputnik changed that all overnight.

107
00:08:59,860 --> 00:09:04,080
Surprisingly, we can trace the roots of the American space program

108
00:09:04,081 --> 00:09:07,100
back to a cabbage patch in Auburn, Massachusetts.

109
00:09:07,860 --> 00:09:15,860
On March 16, 1926, physicist Robert Goddard launched the first liquid-propelled rocket in the world.

110
00:09:16,620 --> 00:09:22,960
The liquid oxygen and gasoline-powered cylinder rose a modest 41 feet in the air

111
00:09:22,961 --> 00:09:26,160
before returning to his Aunt Effie's field.

112
00:09:27,640 --> 00:09:31,700
His flight in 1926 was a pathbreaker worldwide.

113
00:09:32,220 --> 00:09:35,600
Lots of people had understood solid rockets for a long time,

114
00:09:36,060 --> 00:09:39,220
Roman candles and things of that nature that we've been familiar with for centuries.

115
00:09:39,800 --> 00:09:45,000
But in order to really do spaceflight, liquid propellants are the key.

116
00:09:45,860 --> 00:09:52,960
Only liquid propellants can be throttled, providing different amounts of thrust at different stages of a rocket's flight.

117
00:09:53,960 --> 00:09:59,940
Goddard continued his experiments in Roswell, New Mexico during the 30s and early 40s.

118
00:10:00,900 --> 00:10:06,740
Eventually, his rockets achieved altitudes of one mile, but his vision went much further.

119
00:10:08,320 --> 00:10:14,180
In his early days, Goddard had told a member of the press that a rocket could be built to reach the moon.

120
00:10:14,860 --> 00:10:20,460
He was ridiculed in scientific circles and by the man on the street for such a preposterous idea.

121
00:10:21,360 --> 00:10:25,500
The wide open spaces of New Mexico provided a haven

122
00:10:25,501 --> 00:10:30,440
where he could continue to work on his dream away from the public spotlight.

123
00:10:31,620 --> 00:10:38,320
However, because of his death in 1945, he never witnessed an object being launched into outer space.

124
00:10:38,321 --> 00:10:45,240
The next major contribution to the American space program

125
00:10:45,241 --> 00:10:47,500
came from a very unlikely place

126
00:10:47,501 --> 00:10:49,140
Nazi Germany

127
00:10:49,141 --> 00:10:52,180
Lead scientists Werner von Braun

128
00:10:52,181 --> 00:10:56,120
and about 120 other German scientists and engineers

129
00:10:56,121 --> 00:10:59,960
fled from their rocket testing facility in Piena Munde, Germany

130
00:10:59,961 --> 00:11:04,100
and were escorted from Austria back to the United States

131
00:11:04,101 --> 00:11:06,040
in the final days of World War II

132
00:11:06,041 --> 00:11:07,800
by U.S. Army personnel

133
00:11:07,801 --> 00:11:14,522
Von Braun had been fascinated by rockets from an early age.

134
00:11:14,523 --> 00:11:17,940
Now he could use his knowledge

135
00:11:17,941 --> 00:11:21,238
to explore space rather than delivering bombs.

136
00:11:21,239 --> 00:11:24,320
In fact, during World War II, von Braun had

137
00:11:24,321 --> 00:11:30,380
been jailed by the Gestapo for making statements about his desire to work on space travel projects.

138
00:11:30,381 --> 00:11:39,380
For the Nazis, anything less than 100% devotion to the V-2 superweapon was seen as malicious sabotage.

139
00:11:40,740 --> 00:11:47,240
However, since von Braun was so important to the German war effort, he remained in jail for only two weeks.

140
00:11:49,740 --> 00:11:50,440
The U.S.

141
00:11:50,441 --> 00:11:57,600
brought back several of the 29,000-pound V-2 rockets that Hitler's army had been using to terrorize Europe during the war.

142
00:12:00,380 --> 00:12:09,980
Soon, these former enemies were using their one-time secret weapons to conduct groundbreaking experiments for the American military.

143
00:12:10,480 --> 00:12:15,241
However, the propulsion unit initially chosen to power the first U.S.

144
00:12:15,242 --> 00:12:22,900
satellite, the three-pound Vanguard I, was supplied by the Navy instead of von Braun's team of Army scientists.

145
00:12:24,500 --> 00:12:29,220
On December 6, 1957, the Navy got their chance.

146
00:12:30,380 --> 00:12:38,040
Dozens of newsreel cameras and hundreds of reporters

147
00:12:38,041 --> 00:12:41,880
witnessed the United States' first attempt to catch up in the space race

148
00:12:41,881 --> 00:12:43,540
go up in flames.

149
00:12:45,580 --> 00:12:50,460
The government gave Werner von Braun a chance to redeem the sagging space program

150
00:12:50,461 --> 00:12:55,340
and von Braun gave Gordon Vaith a sneak peek at the rocket that would make it possible.

151
00:12:56,560 --> 00:13:00,240
Von Braun took me one day down into a shed down at Huntsville.

152
00:13:00,241 --> 00:13:05,967
Alabama at the Army Ballistic Missile Agency and he showed me a rocket assembly.

153
00:13:05,968 --> 00:13:06,668
He said,

154
00:13:06,669 --> 00:13:07,673
there it is.

155
00:13:07,673 --> 00:13:15,040
When the government says we need a rocket assembly to launch a satellite, that's it.

156
00:13:15,380 --> 00:13:17,038
We're ready to go.

157
00:13:17,038 --> 00:13:24,220
Von Braun watched on January 31st, 1958, as the Army tried to launch the first

158
00:13:24,221 --> 00:13:29,220
American satellite Explore-1 nearly four months after Sputnik.

159
00:13:37,380 --> 00:13:43,820
The 31-pound Explore-1 successfully entered orbit and remained in space eight years longer

160
00:13:43,821 --> 00:13:44,799
than Sputnik.

161
00:13:44,800 --> 00:13:50,600
After the launch, von Braun declared, we have firmly established our foothold

162
00:13:50,601 --> 00:13:54,600
in space. We will never give it up again.

163
00:13:57,060 --> 00:13:58,780
America launched dozens

164
00:13:58,781 --> 00:14:03,040
of other scientific explorer satellites over the next 17 years,

165
00:14:03,440 --> 00:14:08,020
most of them under the supervision of the government's newly formed space agency, NASA.

166
00:14:09,100 --> 00:14:12,980
Just eight months after Explorer 1, the National Aeronautics

167
00:14:12,981 --> 00:14:17,060
and Space Administration came into being on October 1, 1958.

168
00:14:17,061 --> 00:14:22,840
Its official purpose was the peaceful exploration of space.

169
00:14:23,460 --> 00:14:27,780
However, its practical goal during this stage of the Cold War

170
00:14:27,781 --> 00:14:31,880
was to catch up to and surpass the Soviet Union in the space race.

171
00:14:33,640 --> 00:14:37,320
But in 1961, the Soviets upped the ante.

172
00:14:37,960 --> 00:14:42,000
On April 12th, the Soviet Union launched a very different type of satellite.

173
00:14:42,780 --> 00:14:44,660
One with a man inside.

174
00:14:47,060 --> 00:14:53,760
Yuri Gagarin became the first human being in space and the first person to orbit the Earth.

175
00:14:54,100 --> 00:15:02,540
After reaching a maximum height of 203 miles, he landed in a field in the Soviet Union an hour and 48 minutes later,

176
00:15:03,160 --> 00:15:06,980
giving the communists another worldwide propaganda victory.

177
00:15:09,800 --> 00:15:12,540
After the historic flight, Gagarin said,

178
00:15:12,541 --> 00:15:15,340
I certainly did not feel lonely at all,

179
00:15:15,800 --> 00:15:19,200
for I knew that my friends, in fact all the Soviet people,

180
00:15:19,500 --> 00:15:21,420
were watching my spaceflight.

181
00:15:24,340 --> 00:15:29,460
Ten months later, John Glenn became America's first human satellite.

182
00:15:31,040 --> 00:15:32,560
Godspeed, John Glenn.

183
00:15:35,260 --> 00:15:39,740
On February 20th, 1962, he circled the Earth three times

184
00:15:39,741 --> 00:15:42,560
at 17,500 miles an hour.

185
00:15:42,561 --> 00:15:44,760
Roger, zero G, and I feel fine.

186
00:15:44,900 --> 00:15:46,220
Capsule is turning around.

187
00:15:46,780 --> 00:15:48,440
Oh, that view is tremendous.

188
00:15:49,100 --> 00:15:52,180
Several days later, Glenn received a hero's welcome

189
00:15:52,181 --> 00:15:54,940
during a New York City ticker tape parade.

190
00:15:56,960 --> 00:16:00,520
This has been a great experience for all of us on the program

191
00:16:00,521 --> 00:16:02,700
and for all Americans, I guess, too.

192
00:16:04,060 --> 00:16:07,240
And I'm certainly glad to see that pride in our country

193
00:16:07,241 --> 00:16:10,040
and its accomplishments are not a thing of the past.

194
00:16:11,340 --> 00:16:14,900
But the United States had finished in second place again

195
00:16:14,901 --> 00:16:17,780
and many people wondered if the country could really meet

196
00:16:17,781 --> 00:16:20,060
President John F. Kennedy's challenge.

197
00:16:21,060 --> 00:16:23,940
I believe that this nation should commit itself

198
00:16:23,941 --> 00:16:27,980
to achieving the goal before this decade is out

199
00:16:27,981 --> 00:16:32,160
of landing a man on the moon and returning him safely to the earth.

200
00:16:33,440 --> 00:16:37,220
Billions of dollars continued to pour into the U.S. space program.

201
00:16:38,080 --> 00:16:50,860
Finally, on July 20th, 1969, with the help of a 363-foot-tall, 6.3 million-pound Saturn V rocket, America finished first.

202
00:16:56,560 --> 00:17:02,520
That's one small step for man, one giant leap for mankind.

203
00:17:02,521 --> 00:17:07,440
The US had won the grandest public prize in the space race.

204
00:17:08,540 --> 00:17:10,820
And national confidence had been restored.

205
00:17:11,580 --> 00:17:14,300
But an even more crucial race continued.

206
00:17:15,720 --> 00:17:18,520
Reconnaissance satellites assumed critical importance

207
00:17:18,521 --> 00:17:22,040
as the superpowers continued to build their nuclear arsenals.

208
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:28,520
If one side could pinpoint the locations of the other's missiles using satellite photos,

209
00:17:28,521 --> 00:17:31,360
then a lethal first strike would be possible.

210
00:17:32,360 --> 00:17:34,980
Effective reconnaissance equaled preparedness,

211
00:17:35,720 --> 00:17:40,580
and a lack of preparedness could result in complete annihilation.

212
00:17:43,880 --> 00:17:49,760
On November 3, 1957, Sputnik 2 was the first satellite to carry a passenger,

213
00:17:50,320 --> 00:17:52,260
a small dog named Laika.

214
00:17:52,760 --> 00:17:58,180
He died after about a week, but proved that life is possible in space.

215
00:17:59,000 --> 00:18:02,440
Satellites will continue in a moment on Modern Marvels.

216
00:18:04,040 --> 00:18:07,460
While NASA conducted its highly publicized missions,

217
00:18:07,840 --> 00:18:10,840
the darker covert side of the space race continued.

218
00:18:11,760 --> 00:18:14,840
The American military was working in great secrecy

219
00:18:14,841 --> 00:18:19,080
to design reconnaissance satellites to spy on their communist rival.

220
00:18:20,920 --> 00:18:22,960
Determining the number of Soviet missiles

221
00:18:22,961 --> 00:18:26,780
and the strength and location of its troops was vital information

222
00:18:26,781 --> 00:18:31,520
that could prevent the Cold War from spiraling out of control.

223
00:18:32,740 --> 00:18:37,180
But developing the technology to take detailed photographs from space

224
00:18:37,181 --> 00:18:40,740
was proving to be an extremely difficult challenge.

225
00:18:43,420 --> 00:18:47,640
The first attempt was the 1,300-pound Discoverer I,

226
00:18:47,641 --> 00:18:52,860
finally launched on February 28, 1959, after many delays.

227
00:18:52,861 --> 00:18:57,640
The government told the public it was a scientific orbiter

228
00:18:57,641 --> 00:19:04,560
But Discoverer was simply a cover name for the CIA spy satellite project codenamed Corona

229
00:19:04,561 --> 00:19:09,640
Discoverer 1 did not carry a military payload

230
00:19:09,641 --> 00:19:13,720
But it was intended to eventually prove out the technology

231
00:19:13,721 --> 00:19:17,480
It ended up flying into the snow in Antarctica

232
00:19:17,481 --> 00:19:20,720
And we then went on to Discoverer 2, which failed

233
00:19:20,721 --> 00:19:22,680
And 3, which failed, and 4, which failed

234
00:19:22,681 --> 00:19:28,840
And we had continuous failures with these early military satellites until Discoverer 13.

235
00:19:29,520 --> 00:19:38,740
On August 11, 1960, Discoverer 13 ejected a capsule covered by a heat shield, which plummeted towards Earth.

236
00:19:39,440 --> 00:19:45,060
A Navy helicopter recovered it from the Pacific Ocean and quickly flew it to Washington, D.C.,

237
00:19:45,061 --> 00:19:50,780
where the capsule's prize was presented to President Eisenhower, an American flag.

238
00:19:52,680 --> 00:20:00,320
Although Discoverer 13 had proven that an object could be brought back from outer space,

239
00:20:00,980 --> 00:20:06,880
top-secret photographs were not recovered until an Air Force plane literally plucked the parachute

240
00:20:06,881 --> 00:20:13,240
attached to Discoverer 14's film capsule out of the sky on August 19, 1960.

241
00:20:16,600 --> 00:20:22,160
It returned film of the Soviet Union, and it excited a lot of people within the intelligence community

242
00:20:22,161 --> 00:20:24,680
that the day of satellite reconnaissance was here.

243
00:20:25,660 --> 00:20:29,240
Although the CIA won't release altitude figures

244
00:20:29,241 --> 00:20:31,140
for modern reconnaissance orbiters,

245
00:20:31,640 --> 00:20:34,540
we do know that the Discoverer spy satellites

246
00:20:34,541 --> 00:20:37,300
operated in a low-Earth elliptical orbit,

247
00:20:37,800 --> 00:20:41,220
generally between 100 and 400 miles above the Earth.

248
00:20:42,320 --> 00:20:45,000
They flew over the poles in order to take pictures

249
00:20:45,001 --> 00:20:46,620
of many different locations

250
00:20:46,621 --> 00:20:50,460
as the world rotated underneath their polar path.

251
00:20:52,160 --> 00:20:57,800
However, the lag time between when a picture was taken by these early discoverer satellites

252
00:20:57,801 --> 00:21:03,300
and when a CIA analyst could look at the finished photo after the film was recovered,

253
00:21:03,800 --> 00:21:07,800
sent back to Washington, D.C. and developed, was about two weeks.

254
00:21:08,420 --> 00:21:10,080
Hardly instant analysis.

255
00:21:10,920 --> 00:21:14,780
But technicians hoped that the new satellite images would at least be an improvement

256
00:21:14,781 --> 00:21:18,020
over existing U-2 spy plane photos.

257
00:21:18,021 --> 00:21:27,040
photo interpreters who were using the u2 imagery were told to put that imagery aside and when they

258
00:21:27,041 --> 00:21:32,520
got the new film from the satellites they looked at it and they were rather disappointed it was not

259
00:21:32,521 --> 00:21:37,620
as good a resolution as they expected and so they actually went back and pulled out their u2

260
00:21:37,621 --> 00:21:45,380
photography again but two events in the early 1960s convinced experts that the long-term success of

261
00:21:45,381 --> 00:21:48,540
the military satellite reconnaissance program was crucial.

262
00:21:49,900 --> 00:21:57,380
On May 1st, 1960, Soviet surface-to-air missiles shot down Air Force pilot Francis Gary Powers'

263
00:21:57,560 --> 00:22:01,740
U-2 spy plane 80,000 feet above the Soviet Union.

264
00:22:04,260 --> 00:22:05,780
Powers survived.

265
00:22:06,740 --> 00:22:13,640
But in October 1962, during the Cuban Missile Crisis, the Soviets shot down another U-2 airplane,

266
00:22:13,641 --> 00:22:16,220
and this time the pilot was killed.

267
00:22:17,440 --> 00:22:20,000
It was obvious that better satellite images

268
00:22:20,001 --> 00:22:22,380
would allow vital information to be gathered

269
00:22:22,381 --> 00:22:25,140
without risking more American lives.

270
00:22:26,780 --> 00:22:31,300
An indication of America's commitment to space-based surveillance

271
00:22:31,301 --> 00:22:33,800
was the establishment in August 1960

272
00:22:33,801 --> 00:22:36,680
of the National Reconnaissance Office.

273
00:22:37,600 --> 00:22:40,060
Its very existence was classified

274
00:22:40,061 --> 00:22:47,180
and its job was to spy on the Soviet Union as well as China, Libya and other countries.

275
00:22:48,800 --> 00:22:57,540
In 1962, all of the individual reconnaissance satellite projects in the NRO fell under the general codename of Keyhole.

276
00:23:00,040 --> 00:23:06,680
During the 1960s and early 1970s, more efficient satellites containing several film capsules

277
00:23:06,681 --> 00:23:10,300
and a more streamlined development and analysis process

278
00:23:10,301 --> 00:23:13,580
reduced the delay time to three or four days.

279
00:23:15,220 --> 00:23:20,040
But film was still physically sent back from space in canisters

280
00:23:20,041 --> 00:23:25,140
because several experiments to transmit images using radio signals had failed.

281
00:23:27,140 --> 00:23:31,580
Two world events proved that a several-day delay was still too long.

282
00:23:31,581 --> 00:23:37,080
on August 21st 1968

283
00:23:37,081 --> 00:23:40,360
Soviet troops invaded Czechoslovakia

284
00:23:40,361 --> 00:23:42,820
to suppress a pro-democracy movement

285
00:23:42,821 --> 00:23:44,800
centered in the capital of Prague

286
00:23:44,801 --> 00:23:47,760
and on October 6th 1973

287
00:23:47,761 --> 00:23:51,220
Egypt and Syria launched a surprise attack on Israel

288
00:23:51,221 --> 00:23:56,700
in both cases

289
00:23:56,701 --> 00:23:59,940
keyhole satellites did not return photographs

290
00:23:59,941 --> 00:24:02,820
until several days after the invasions had occurred.

291
00:24:03,540 --> 00:24:05,160
We had wonderful coverage,

292
00:24:05,600 --> 00:24:09,200
but we didn't get the photographs until the war was over.

293
00:24:13,980 --> 00:24:18,240
On January 20, 1977, a brisk winter day,

294
00:24:18,860 --> 00:24:21,240
Jimmy Carter walked down Pennsylvania Avenue

295
00:24:21,241 --> 00:24:24,820
following his inauguration as President of the United States.

296
00:24:25,520 --> 00:24:29,140
However, another exciting event was also taking place

297
00:24:29,141 --> 00:24:30,980
just a few miles away.

298
00:24:32,180 --> 00:24:35,940
That same afternoon, the newest model of Keyhole satellite

299
00:24:35,941 --> 00:24:39,460
was able to transmit the first nearly real-time images

300
00:24:39,461 --> 00:24:42,680
from outer space to the NRO Processing Center

301
00:24:42,681 --> 00:24:44,220
at Fort Belvoir, Virginia.

302
00:24:45,520 --> 00:24:49,180
Each dot of the photo or pixel was assigned a number

303
00:24:49,181 --> 00:24:52,560
corresponding to white, black, or varying shades of gray.

304
00:24:53,960 --> 00:24:56,720
These numbers were converted into a signal

305
00:24:56,721 --> 00:25:01,580
and beamed to Earth where the digital transmission was converted back into a photo.

306
00:25:03,180 --> 00:25:07,020
President Carter did not see the images until the next day,

307
00:25:07,440 --> 00:25:13,560
but he immediately recognized their value in making critical decisions based on photographic facts

308
00:25:13,561 --> 00:25:15,880
instead of the theories of analysts.

309
00:25:16,940 --> 00:25:23,020
Keyhole Model 11 had just reduced the lag time for spy satellites from several days to a matter of minutes.

310
00:25:23,021 --> 00:25:33,360
But very few people in the government were allowed to see the top secret images from this new keyhole satellite, also known as KH-11.

311
00:25:34,740 --> 00:25:42,380
Even today, only satellite photos taken before 1972 have been declassified and made available to the public.

312
00:25:43,020 --> 00:25:47,820
However, this secret information has been prone to leaks over the years.

313
00:25:47,821 --> 00:25:59,120
In 1978, William Campiles, a former CIA employee, sold the entire KH-1 manual to the Soviets for $3,000.

314
00:25:59,820 --> 00:26:02,540
He received a 40-year prison sentence.

315
00:26:03,780 --> 00:26:05,318
Then in 1984, a U.S.

316
00:26:05,319 --> 00:26:11,700
naval intelligence analyst leaked this KH-11 image to an English military magazine.

317
00:26:11,701 --> 00:26:18,180
It shows the first nuclear-powered Soviet aircraft carrier during construction.

318
00:26:19,280 --> 00:26:23,760
Samuel Long Morrison received a two-year sentence for leaking the photo.

319
00:26:27,740 --> 00:26:31,700
Since all modern information and images are still classified,

320
00:26:32,960 --> 00:26:36,280
the spot commercial photographic satellite developed in France

321
00:26:36,281 --> 00:26:41,120
gives us some idea of military satellite reconnaissance capabilities.

322
00:26:41,700 --> 00:26:51,700
It is used for a variety of business purposes including urban planning, crop yield assessments and environmental issues such as gauging deforestation.

323
00:26:51,701 --> 00:26:57,700
Still, the SPOT satellite is not nearly as accurate as military systems.

324
00:26:57,701 --> 00:27:07,700
SPOT's resolution capacity is 33 feet, whereas experts believe today's military models may have resolutions of only a few inches.

325
00:27:07,701 --> 00:27:13,180
But very few people in the government know the exact resolution numbers.

326
00:27:13,700 --> 00:27:19,540
When you measure resolution, you are actually not measuring the smallest object you are seeing.

327
00:27:20,120 --> 00:27:24,780
You measure the smallest distance that you can determine between two objects.

328
00:27:25,360 --> 00:27:29,280
You cannot read license plates from a satellite in orbit.

329
00:27:30,060 --> 00:27:35,320
They have a resolution that is probably about the equivalent of a softball, about six inches.

330
00:27:35,321 --> 00:27:40,540
if two objects are about a softball apart, you can tell that from orbit.

331
00:27:42,280 --> 00:27:47,060
New reconnaissance systems even use infrared and ultraviolet sources

332
00:27:47,061 --> 00:27:50,340
instead of relying solely on the visible light spectrum.

333
00:27:51,260 --> 00:27:56,600
This means that these satellites can still obtain images on cloudy days or even at night.

334
00:27:58,600 --> 00:28:03,420
But the research and development devoted to American military satellites

335
00:28:03,421 --> 00:28:06,940
has also spawned a new wave of high-tech civilian uses.

336
00:28:07,540 --> 00:28:13,900
In fact, one type of satellite routinely saves thousands of lives every year.

337
00:28:15,400 --> 00:28:20,260
Some scientists believe that global warming will result in more frequent

338
00:28:20,261 --> 00:28:23,660
and more powerful natural disasters in the coming years.

339
00:28:24,500 --> 00:28:30,160
In the past, commercial and residential property losses due to tornadoes, floods, and hurricanes

340
00:28:30,161 --> 00:28:36,360
have often escalated into the hundreds of millions and sometimes billions of dollars.

341
00:28:37,260 --> 00:28:42,860
Yet there is one figure that has been decreasing worldwide over the last few decades.

342
00:28:43,520 --> 00:28:44,220
Fatalities.

343
00:28:45,360 --> 00:28:48,180
Like many other practical forms of technology,

344
00:28:48,860 --> 00:28:52,980
America's first weather satellite emerged from a military project.

345
00:28:54,200 --> 00:28:59,020
TIROS-1, which was launched April 1, 1960,

346
00:28:59,021 --> 00:29:03,100
grew out of a study that one of the companies had made

347
00:29:03,101 --> 00:29:06,580
about the possible use for reconnaissance satellite.

348
00:29:07,020 --> 00:29:11,040
It had a television camera and produced images of clouds.

349
00:29:12,000 --> 00:29:15,080
The images Tyrus-1 beamed back to Earth

350
00:29:15,081 --> 00:29:18,100
would never have sufficed for military reconnaissance,

351
00:29:18,740 --> 00:29:22,840
but the ability to see cloud formations over large regions of the Earth

352
00:29:22,841 --> 00:29:26,860
had an immediate impact on weather forecasting and storm warnings.

353
00:29:26,861 --> 00:29:36,100
In fact, Tyrus III, launched just four years after Sputnik in 1961, identified Hurricane

354
00:29:36,101 --> 00:29:42,580
Esther, forming in the Atlantic Ocean more than two days before any other means of observation.

355
00:29:43,120 --> 00:29:48,480
If there's a hurricane bearing down on you, or there's a severe storm situation developing

356
00:29:48,481 --> 00:29:52,960
in the Midwest where tornadoes are going to be generated, the ability to give warnings

357
00:29:52,961 --> 00:29:57,540
that this is going to happen is the first step in saving lives.

358
00:29:58,120 --> 00:30:01,420
That's where the weather satellite comes in, particularly strong.

359
00:30:02,540 --> 00:30:06,000
Weather satellites designed to take high-resolution photos

360
00:30:06,001 --> 00:30:08,400
and gather precise atmospheric data

361
00:30:08,401 --> 00:30:11,940
generally operate in low Earth polar orbits,

362
00:30:12,480 --> 00:30:14,820
about 200 to 400 miles high.

363
00:30:15,380 --> 00:30:18,620
However, the cloud pictures covering large regions of the Earth

364
00:30:18,621 --> 00:30:21,480
that most people are familiar with from weather forecasts

365
00:30:21,481 --> 00:30:26,680
are taken from geostationary orbit over 22,000 miles away,

366
00:30:27,380 --> 00:30:31,840
the same orbit Arthur C. Clarke advocated in 1945.

367
00:30:33,600 --> 00:30:38,580
At this distance, the satellite revolves at the same speed as the Earth rotates,

368
00:30:39,000 --> 00:30:42,060
continuously remaining over the same spot on the globe.

369
00:30:43,000 --> 00:30:47,860
Before the 1960s, reconnaissance aircraft often couldn't spot hurricanes

370
00:30:47,861 --> 00:30:53,220
forming far out in the Atlantic until they were hours away from slamming into Caribbean

371
00:30:53,221 --> 00:30:58,040
islands or the southeastern United States.

372
00:30:58,041 --> 00:31:04,900
Thanks to the global coverage of weather satellites, people now have days to evacuate.

373
00:31:04,901 --> 00:31:10,600
And fewer doubters stay behind to ride it out since they can see the huge swirling images

374
00:31:10,601 --> 00:31:17,000
and hear the accurate data about wind speeds and rainfall amounts on their own TV screens.

375
00:31:19,060 --> 00:31:24,500
Today's weather satellites are also capable of much more than taking pictures of clouds.

376
00:31:25,260 --> 00:31:30,480
They provide high-resolution images for extremely accurate three-dimensional maps

377
00:31:30,481 --> 00:31:36,140
and gather incredibly precise data about global weather systems in the upper atmosphere.

378
00:31:36,141 --> 00:31:42,380
They are even equipped with a search and rescue feature for people traveling in remote parts of the world.

379
00:31:43,720 --> 00:31:50,400
In addition to the most common use, which is for boats, it's used for downed aircraft,

380
00:31:50,800 --> 00:31:55,600
where if they are in serious trouble, they can activate the radio transmitter

381
00:31:55,601 --> 00:32:00,620
and it sends out a radio transmission that is received by the satellite.

382
00:32:00,621 --> 00:32:08,000
And that then is relayed to Coast Guard or whoever to launch a search and try to rescue them.

383
00:32:08,340 --> 00:32:10,440
And it has been extremely successful.

384
00:32:10,680 --> 00:32:13,820
They measure the number of saves in hundreds.

385
00:32:15,180 --> 00:32:22,740
Besides weather satellites, Earth Resources orbiters are also helping researchers learn more about our planet.

386
00:32:23,560 --> 00:32:27,620
The Landsat family of satellites take a variety of high-resolution images,

387
00:32:27,621 --> 00:32:34,280
images which can be transformed by computers into 3D animations to assess crop yields,

388
00:32:34,920 --> 00:32:41,900
identify fault lines, aid in urban planning, and manage other natural resources such as forests.

389
00:32:43,060 --> 00:32:48,860
Because satellites can provide accurate positioning information, a growing number of consumer

390
00:32:48,861 --> 00:32:55,680
applications are taking advantage of this technology. The Global Positioning System,

391
00:32:55,681 --> 00:33:01,940
or GPS uses four or more satellites to fix a transmitter's position on the earth to within

392
00:33:01,941 --> 00:33:02,987
a few yards.

393
00:33:02,988 --> 00:33:09,440
Such accuracy allows small boats and rental cars to navigate by satellite.

394
00:33:10,660 --> 00:33:16,860
We could pull up a screen in a car and see exactly where we're located and plot the course

395
00:33:16,861 --> 00:33:19,972
to where we need to go. I don't know about you, but I get lost all the time.

396
00:33:19,973 --> 00:33:20,673
And as my

397
00:33:20,674 --> 00:33:23,084
wife always says, I don't want to ask for directions.

398
00:33:23,085 --> 00:33:24,740
This enables me to do it without

399
00:33:24,741 --> 00:33:29,740
without asking anybody else except a satellite.

400
00:33:29,741 --> 00:33:32,740
While man-made orbiters fulfill a variety of functions

401
00:33:32,741 --> 00:33:36,740
such as weather forecasting, navigation, or military reconnaissance,

402
00:33:36,741 --> 00:33:39,740
they are all in a sense communication satellites

403
00:33:39,741 --> 00:33:43,740
because if they cannot send images or data back to Earth,

404
00:33:43,741 --> 00:33:48,740
then they are simply pieces of space junk.

405
00:33:48,741 --> 00:33:52,740
Coming up, from a crude aluminized balloon

406
00:33:52,741 --> 00:33:57,820
High-tech machines that can handle 120,000 phone calls simultaneously.

407
00:33:58,760 --> 00:34:04,340
The extraordinary evolution of communication satellites has rapidly changed our world.

408
00:34:06,220 --> 00:34:09,180
Satellites are seen by reflected sunlight.

409
00:34:09,660 --> 00:34:12,120
The best time to look for them is early evening.

410
00:34:13,580 --> 00:34:15,500
Modern marvels will return.

411
00:34:15,501 --> 00:34:24,820
Like scientific, military, and weather satellites, communication satellites also evolved from humble beginnings.

412
00:34:25,500 --> 00:34:34,500
In fact, the first communication satellite Echo-1 launched in 1960 was nothing more than a 100-foot-wide aluminized balloon.

413
00:34:36,040 --> 00:34:43,980
It was a passive satellite that one could point a beam at and bounce a radio beacon off of to someplace else.

414
00:34:43,981 --> 00:34:49,580
you could actually see this thing in orbit as it flew over because it was so large and it reflected so nicely.

415
00:34:49,920 --> 00:34:54,940
President Eisenhower listened to a test of his own voice being beamed across the country.

416
00:34:55,080 --> 00:35:02,100
In this first experiment in communications involving the use of a satellite balloon known as ECHO,

417
00:35:03,260 --> 00:35:09,080
this is one more significant step in the United States program of space...

418
00:35:09,081 --> 00:35:13,820
However, most people realized this was hardly the most effective means of communication.

419
00:35:13,980 --> 00:35:20,980
In 1962, AT&T developed Telstar, the world's first commercial satellite.

420
00:35:20,981 --> 00:35:23,980
It could actively receive and retransmit signals.

421
00:35:23,981 --> 00:35:26,980
But it operated in a low Earth orbit.

422
00:35:26,981 --> 00:35:31,980
This meant that it had to be tracked by moving satellite dishes on the ground.

423
00:35:31,981 --> 00:35:36,980
If any problem occurred during tracking, the communication link would be lost.

424
00:35:36,981 --> 00:35:42,980
In addition, Telstar was only available over the United States for a few minutes at a time

425
00:35:42,981 --> 00:35:45,920
at a time before quickly circling the rest of the globe.

426
00:35:47,680 --> 00:35:55,240
Then, in 1963, the Hughes Corporation launched the much smaller 150-pound Syncom-1,

427
00:35:55,840 --> 00:35:59,500
the world's first geostationary communication satellite.

428
00:35:59,940 --> 00:36:04,240
It could carry only 240 telephone calls at a time.

429
00:36:04,241 --> 00:36:09,000
But since Syncom was over 22,000 miles above the equator,

430
00:36:09,001 --> 00:36:11,960
it revolved at the same rate as the Earth,

431
00:36:12,080 --> 00:36:14,260
which eliminated both the need for tracking

432
00:36:14,261 --> 00:36:17,180
and the problem of intermittent availability.

433
00:36:18,980 --> 00:36:23,320
Even though Syncom was a relatively lightweight satellite by today's times

434
00:36:23,321 --> 00:36:25,060
and used relatively simple technology,

435
00:36:25,580 --> 00:36:30,120
it did enable John F. Kennedy to make a telephone call

436
00:36:30,121 --> 00:36:34,780
from Washington, D.C. to a ship off the coast of Africa.

437
00:36:34,781 --> 00:36:41,780
This first test proved that Arthur C. Clarke's visionary concept from 1945 worked,

438
00:36:41,781 --> 00:36:47,780
and geostationary orbit became the standard location for most communication satellites.

439
00:36:47,781 --> 00:36:54,780
Today, more than 150 of these modern marvels operate in this position serving almost every country on Earth.

440
00:36:58,780 --> 00:37:02,780
These pioneering efforts made the formation of Intelsat possible.

441
00:37:02,781 --> 00:37:04,780
and Telsat possible.

442
00:37:04,781 --> 00:37:08,780
This is an international organization advocated by President Kennedy,

443
00:37:08,781 --> 00:37:13,780
designed to link all the nations of the world by satellite communications.

444
00:37:13,781 --> 00:37:19,780
Intelsat-1, also built by Hughes, was launched in 1965.

445
00:37:24,780 --> 00:37:26,780
The effort was a great success,

446
00:37:26,781 --> 00:37:31,780
and today there are over 20 Intelsat orbiters bringing our world together.

447
00:37:32,780 --> 00:37:39,080
Modern communication satellites are made with some of the lightest and strongest materials on Earth.

448
00:37:40,260 --> 00:37:49,620
The graphite epoxy material that we're using in our satellites to keep the weight down is about half the weight of aluminum and ten times the strength.

449
00:37:50,280 --> 00:37:58,160
In order to keep liquid fuel weights down, solar panels are used to power most of the onboard electronic components.

450
00:37:58,161 --> 00:38:05,760
Usually smaller satellites requiring less solar power are built in a drum shape about 21 feet tall.

451
00:38:06,660 --> 00:38:15,280
Larger satellites use expansive solar wings, which are unfolded in orbit, leading to widths of more than 85 feet.

452
00:38:22,880 --> 00:38:26,560
Satellites have also dramatically affected our culture.

453
00:38:26,561 --> 00:38:30,080
by relaying television signals around the globe.

454
00:38:31,180 --> 00:38:33,920
The 1964 Olympics were in Japan,

455
00:38:34,560 --> 00:38:37,880
and for the first time using CINCOM,

456
00:38:37,881 --> 00:38:42,660
we were able to see the Olympics in the United States

457
00:38:42,661 --> 00:38:45,100
transmitted live from Japan,

458
00:38:46,040 --> 00:38:49,420
and that brought home loud and clear

459
00:38:49,421 --> 00:38:52,840
how important satellite communications could be

460
00:38:52,841 --> 00:38:53,940
for television distribution.

461
00:38:53,941 --> 00:39:02,580
Satellite television transmissions in the 1980s allowed the people of the Soviet Union to see how the Western world lived.

462
00:39:03,520 --> 00:39:09,880
And today, Chinese citizens also have increasing access to news and information from other parts of the world.

463
00:39:11,220 --> 00:39:13,178
The world will never be the same.

464
00:39:13,179 --> 00:39:18,340
There is no longer any remote places in this world because of satellite communications.

465
00:39:18,341 --> 00:39:24,340
Thanks to stronger amplification of television signals by today's satellites,

466
00:39:24,341 --> 00:39:29,340
consumers can now receive broadcasts directly on 18-inch wide dishes.

467
00:39:30,340 --> 00:39:37,340
As improvements continue, these satellite receivers may become an integral part of most households,

468
00:39:37,341 --> 00:39:44,340
providing 400 channels of incoming television and data, as well as one channel of outgoing video.

469
00:39:44,341 --> 00:39:51,340
This outgoing channel will make video conferences as easy as phone calls are today.

470
00:39:51,341 --> 00:39:58,340
Communication satellites have transformed our world into a global village in less than four decades.

471
00:39:58,341 --> 00:40:05,340
Today we can pick up the phone and instantly talk to someone else almost anywhere on the planet.

472
00:40:05,341 --> 00:40:12,340
Or turn on our televisions and watch foreign and domestic events unfold before our eyes.

473
00:40:12,341 --> 00:40:25,069
International faxing, high-speed modems, and email using phone lines and satellites also allow businesses, researchers, and ordinary people to transmit data around the

474
00:40:25,070 --> 00:40:27,280
globe in a matter of seconds.

475
00:40:27,281 --> 00:40:34,960
real-time worldwide communications was something that didn't exist before the 1960s in the

476
00:40:34,961 --> 00:40:38,320
communications satellite revolution that has changed our whole way of life

477
00:40:38,321 --> 00:40:42,800
our whole society our economy is built upon those capabilities

478
00:40:42,801 --> 00:40:55,440
when american explorer and discover satellites were racing against the soviet union 40 years ago

479
00:40:55,441 --> 00:41:01,440
No one ever imagined that the space above our planet might become overcrowded.

480
00:41:01,441 --> 00:41:07,440
Yet this is a growing concern since objects in geostationary orbit need to be positioned

481
00:41:07,441 --> 00:41:11,440
a certain distance from the Earth and over the equator.

482
00:41:11,441 --> 00:41:18,440
They also need to be located about 1,000 miles apart from each other so their signals don't get mixed.

483
00:41:18,441 --> 00:41:22,440
Therefore there are only a certain number of slots left to be occupied,

484
00:41:22,441 --> 00:41:25,600
an issue that the United Nations is looking into.

485
00:41:26,640 --> 00:41:29,680
I believe that with good intelligent planning

486
00:41:29,681 --> 00:41:33,340
and good coordination by the International Telecommunications Union,

487
00:41:33,920 --> 00:41:39,380
there will be enough slots for satellites well into the 21st century.

488
00:41:41,020 --> 00:41:45,340
Another concern as space becomes more crowded is orbital debris,

489
00:41:45,700 --> 00:41:47,660
also known as space junk.

490
00:41:48,360 --> 00:41:51,080
This consists of various sized objects,

491
00:41:51,081 --> 00:41:53,640
such as portions of expanded rocket stages,

492
00:41:54,260 --> 00:41:56,340
small parts that have come off a spacecraft,

493
00:41:56,820 --> 00:41:58,040
and even paint chips.

494
00:41:59,900 --> 00:42:02,660
Each dot on this computer animation

495
00:42:02,661 --> 00:42:05,340
represents an actual piece of space debris.

496
00:42:06,060 --> 00:42:08,800
Many of these items do eventually burn up

497
00:42:08,801 --> 00:42:10,320
in Earth's upper atmosphere.

498
00:42:11,020 --> 00:42:14,100
But this process can sometimes take decades to happen

499
00:42:14,101 --> 00:42:16,380
as their orbits gradually decay.

500
00:42:16,381 --> 00:42:23,380
On the surface, something as small as a paint ship may not seem to be much of a concern.

501
00:42:23,381 --> 00:42:30,380
But since it is traveling 17,500 miles an hour, it can still have a significant impact.

502
00:42:30,381 --> 00:42:37,380
In fact, a speck of paint from a satellite did hit one of the windows of the Challenger space shuttle in 1983,

503
00:42:37,381 --> 00:42:40,380
causing a half-centimeter pit.

504
00:42:40,381 --> 00:42:45,380
Obviously, a larger object could have disastrous results.

505
00:42:45,381 --> 00:42:53,040
It does exist. It's up there. It's not a major problem, at least at the present.

506
00:42:53,520 --> 00:42:57,660
I would be more concerned about meteorites and things of that nature.

507
00:42:58,480 --> 00:43:06,360
Despite these concerns, satellites are bringing our world together at a faster pace than any other form of technology.

508
00:43:07,160 --> 00:43:11,020
The term live via satellite used to denote a special world event.

509
00:43:11,021 --> 00:43:16,280
However, today the operation of satellites goes largely unnoticed.

510
00:43:17,980 --> 00:43:22,080
The possibility for the future in satellites is almost limitless.

511
00:43:22,360 --> 00:43:25,220
Whatever we can conceive, we can probably do.

512
00:43:26,580 --> 00:43:34,320
Because of the visionary work of pioneers who stake their careers and their reputations on an unproven concept,

513
00:43:35,080 --> 00:43:38,480
satellites have transformed our world in just 40 years.

514
00:43:38,481 --> 00:43:42,540
from three-pound gadgets, which look more like grapefruits than spacecraft,

515
00:43:43,100 --> 00:43:48,120
to incredibly powerful and complex machines weighing thousands of pounds.

516
00:43:48,960 --> 00:43:54,480
Considering the remarkably rapid evolution of satellites that we have witnessed in the past four decades,

517
00:43:55,120 --> 00:44:01,900
it seems that the only certainty for the next four decades is that satellites will continue to amaze us

518
00:44:01,901 --> 00:44:05,980
in ways that we cannot even imagine today.

